 R toward Tossup"], "rationale": "A heavily Democratic environment, favorable early-voting headlines, and the cycle's most credible poll — which shows Taylor up four — offset the district's Republican lean and Wittman's incumbency and fundraising edge, leaving the race essentially even with a slight Democratic tilt.", "watch": "If late-deciding voters break toward the incumbent as they often do here, or outside spending escalates in the final weeks, the race could tip back to Wittman.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Incumbent Rob Wittman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Rob Wittman, seeking another term in a district that leans Republican by three points, faces Shannon Taylor in a race that has tightened sharply since summer. Wittman holds the traditional advantages: incumbency, name recognition, and a cash edge of more than three million dollars. But outside groups have spent heavily against him, and headlines highlight a Medicaid backlash tied to his record.\n\nThe political environment strongly favors Democrats this year, and early-voting numbers are trending their way. Recent polling is thin but pointed: the most credible survey, from The Washington Post, shows Taylor leading by four among likely voters, while Sabato's Crystal Ball and other raters have shifted the race toward Tossup. Prediction markets price it at roughly even. The forecast calls this a coin-flip contest with a slight Democratic edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4506, "margin": -0.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4359, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4341, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4371, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3736, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3951, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4096, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4532, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.638, "